.TH FENV 7 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
fenv \- floating-point environment overview
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The floating-point environment provides functions and macros for querying and
controlling the state of floating-point arithmetic, including exception flags
and rounding modes.
It is specified by ISO C99 (section 7.6) and POSIX.1\-2001.
.PP
The floating-point environment consists of:
.TP
.B fenv_t
An opaque type representing the entire floating-point environment: rounding
mode, exception sticky flags, and (on x87) exception mask bits.
Instances of this type must not be modified directly; use the fenv(3) family
of functions.
.TP
.B fexcept_t
An opaque type representing a snapshot of the floating-point exception flags
(the sticky bits only, not the exception masks).
.TP
.B FE_DFL_ENV
A pointer-typed constant that refers to the default floating-point environment,
which corresponds to round-to-nearest, no exceptions pending, and all exception
traps masked (non-stop mode).
Passing
.B FE_DFL_ENV
to
.BR fesetenv (3)
or
.BR feupdateenv (3)
restores this default state.
.SS Floating-Point Exception Flags
The following macros represent individual floating-point exception flags
(sticky bits).
Each is a distinct power-of-two integer constant suitable for bitwise OR.
.TP
.B FE_INVALID
An invalid operation was performed (for example,
.BR sqrt (\-1)
or
.BR 0.0 / 0.0 ).
The result is a NaN.
.TP
.B FE_DIVBYZERO
A division by zero occurred (for example,
.B 1.0 / 0.0 ).
The result is an infinity.
.TP
.B FE_OVERFLOW
The magnitude of the result exceeds the largest finite representable value.
The result is rounded to an infinity or the largest finite value depending on
the rounding mode.
Raising
.B FE_OVERFLOW
also raises
.BR FE_INEXACT .
.TP
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
The magnitude of the result is smaller than the smallest normalized value and
precision was lost.
Raising
.B FE_UNDERFLOW
also raises
.BR FE_INEXACT .
.TP
.B FE_INEXACT
The result cannot be represented exactly; it was rounded.
.TP
.B FE_ALL_EXCEPT
The bitwise OR of all exception flags listed above.
Passing
.B FE_ALL_EXCEPT
to
.BR feclearexcept (3)
clears every exception flag at once.
.PP
The non-standard extension macro
.B FE_DENORMAL
(Intel x86 Denormal Operand exception) may be available on x86 targets.
It is guarded with:
.PP
.nf
#ifndef __STRICT_ANSI__
#  define FE_DENORMAL  ...
#endif
.fi
.PP
Code that must be strictly portable should not use
.BR FE_DENORMAL .
.SS Rounding Modes
The following macros represent the four rounding directions defined by IEEE 754
and C99.
They are suitable for use with
.BR fegetround (3)
and
.BR fesetround (3).
.TP
.B FE_TONEAREST
Round to the nearest representable value; ties round to even (the default).
.TP
.B FE_DOWNWARD
Round toward negative infinity (floor).
.TP
.B FE_UPWARD
Round toward positive infinity (ceiling).
.TP
.B FE_TOWARDZERO
Round toward zero (truncation).
.SS FENV_ACCESS Pragma
By default, compilers may assume that floating-point flags and rounding modes
are not observed, allowing them to reorder or eliminate floating-point
operations.
To suppress such optimizations when the program inspects or modifies the
floating-point environment, use:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
.fi
.PP
With GCC, the equivalent approach is to compile with
.B \-frounding\-math
or to disable fast-math for a translation unit via:
.PP
.nf
#pragma GCC optimize ("no-fast-math")
.fi
.PP
Failing to enable one of these guards may cause the compiler to produce
incorrect code when the program modifies rounding modes or reads exception
flags.
.SS x87 FPU and SSE/MXCSR
On x86, the floating-point environment resides in two places:
.TP
.B x87 (80-bit extended precision)
The control word (CW, default 0x037F) holds the exception mask and rounding
bits.
The status word (SW) holds the sticky exception flags.
.TP
.B SSE2 MXCSR (32-bit and 64-bit float/double)
The MXCSR register (default 0x1F80) combines both the exception mask and
sticky flags into a single 32-bit register.
.PP
On Substrate x86-32, the fenv functions operate on both the x87 CW/SW and the
MXCSR when SSE is available.
For strict IEEE 754 compliance in 32-bit mode, code should use
.I double
or
.I float
operations (which GCC may route through SSE) rather than relying on 80-bit
x87 extended precision.
.SS Save, Compute, and Restore Pattern
The standard pattern for performing a computation in a clean floating-point
environment without disturbing the caller's environment is:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>

fenv_t saved;
feholdexcept(&saved);    /* save env, clear flags, mask traps */

/* ... perform floating-point computation ... */

/* optionally inspect new exceptions */
if (fetestexcept(FE_OVERFLOW))
    handle_overflow();
feupdateenv(&saved);     /* restore env, re-raise new exceptions */
.fi
.PP
.BR feholdexcept (3)
enters "non-stop mode" (all traps masked), allowing exceptions to accumulate
as sticky flags without generating signals.
.BR feupdateenv (3)
merges any exceptions raised during the computation back into the restored
environment by calling
.BR feraiseexcept (3).
.PP
If the caller does not want to propagate any exceptions raised in the
computation, use
.BR fesetenv (3)
instead of
.BR feupdateenv (3):
.PP
.nf
fesetenv(&saved);        /* restore env, discard new exceptions */
.fi
